// x25519.nv — the Diffie-Hellman half of Curve25519, and the two
// rules an implementation of it must state out loud.
//
// Every body is a `todo()`.  The signatures and the effect rows are
// the design and the compiler checks them as claims; see the README
// for the stamp and for what is deliberately absent.
//
// THE FUNCTION.  RFC 7748 §5 defines X25519 as a scalar multiplication
// on the Montgomery curve `v² = u³ + 486662·u² + u` over
// GF(2²⁵⁵ − 19), computed by a ladder that carries only the `u`
// coordinate.  Everything is 32 bytes, everything is little-endian,
// and there is no point encoding to get wrong: a public key IS a `u`
// coordinate.  That is why this is the exchange everyone reaches for.
//
// THE TWO RULES.  An X25519 implementation has exactly two decisions
// to make that the specification leaves visible, and a caller cannot
// tell which way they went by looking at the bytes.  This package
// states both:
//
//   CLAMPING happens inside every constructor.  RFC 7748 §5 clears the
//   low three bits and bit 255 of a secret scalar and sets bit 254, and
//   this package does it in `secret_key_from_bytes` rather than
//   expecting the caller to.  So `secret_key_bytes` may not return the
//   32 bytes that went in, and the doc comment on it says so — an
//   implementation that clamped on the way OUT instead would agree
//   with this one on every shared secret and disagree on every stored
//   key.
//
//   AN ALL-ZERO SHARED SECRET IS REFUSED.  RFC 7748 §6.1 says the
//   check is optional.  This package makes it, and
//   `X25519SharedSecretIsZero` is the refusal.  The argument is below,
//   on `diffie_hellman`.
//
// BYTES ARE `[u8]`, matching p256-nv and ed25519-nv, so the three
// curve packages have one shape between them.

// `fe25519` — ed25519-nv's field and its scalar clamping — is this
// package's whole dependency and is named in the manifest, but no line
// here calls it yet: every body is a `todo()`, and an import nothing
// uses is a warning.  The `use` arrives with the first body.

// The width of a secret scalar, in bytes.
pub const X25519_SECRET_KEY_BYTES = 32

// The width of a public key, in bytes.  It is a `u` coordinate and
// nothing else — no format byte, no sign bit, no second coordinate.
pub const X25519_PUBLIC_KEY_BYTES = 32

// The width of a shared secret, in bytes.
//
// **It is not a key.**  It is a `u` coordinate with structure in it,
// and RFC 7748 §6.1 says to run it through a key derivation function
// before using it for anything.  `hash_len(HkdfSha256)` bytes out of
// hkdf-nv's `extract` is the shape that gives.
pub const X25519_SHARED_SECRET_BYTES = 32

// A clamped secret scalar.
//
// CLAMPED, which is the whole reason this is a type rather than 32
// bytes: every value of it has had the low three bits and bit 255
// cleared and bit 254 set, because the only constructor does that.  So
// a scalar that exists is one the ladder runs in constant time and one
// that is a multiple of the cofactor.
pub struct X25519SecretKey
    // Exactly `X25519_SECRET_KEY_BYTES` bytes, already clamped.
    scalar: [u8]

// A peer's public key: a `u` coordinate.
//
// Length-checked and nothing more.  There is no validation to do here
// that is worth doing separately — X25519 has no invalid-curve attack
// of the kind P-256's `validate` closes, because the ladder is
// twist-secure: a `u` that is not on the curve is on the quadratic
// twist, whose order is also large enough that nothing leaks.  What
// remains is the low-order points, and `diffie_hellman` catches those
// by refusing the all-zero output rather than by enumerating them.
pub struct X25519PublicKey
    // Exactly `X25519_PUBLIC_KEY_BYTES` bytes, little-endian.
    u: [u8]

// A scalar and the public key it generates.
//
// The two halves are always consistent: there is no constructor that
// takes both, only ones that derive the public half from the secret.
pub struct X25519KeyPair
    // The public half.
    public: X25519PublicKey
    // The secret half, clamped.
    secret: X25519SecretKey

// Why a key or a shared secret could not be produced.
//
// Two of the three are widths.  The third is this package's one
// interface decision that RFC 7748 leaves to the implementer.
pub enum X25519Error
    // A byte list of the wrong width reached a secret-key constructor.
    X25519SecretKeyLengthWrong(got: Int, want: Int)
    // A byte list of the wrong width reached a public-key constructor.
    X25519PublicKeyLengthWrong(got: Int, want: Int)
    // The exchange produced 32 zero bytes, which means the peer's
    // public key was one of the low-order points and the "shared"
    // secret is a constant the peer already knew.  RFC 7748 §6.1 makes
    // this check OPTIONAL; this package makes it.  `diffie_hellman`
    // says why.
    X25519SharedSecretIsZero

// What went wrong, in words, for a caller that logs rather than
// branches.
//
// `Result<_, X25519Error>` requires it: SPEC §3.4 admits a `Result`
// error type only if it implements `Error`, so this impl is what lets
// every constructor below name its own refusal instead of returning
// `Result<_, Str>`.
impl Error for X25519Error
    fn message(self) -> Str
        todo("x25519-nv.x25519.X25519Error.message")

// Read a secret scalar from 32 bytes the CALLER supplies, clamping it.
//
// This package has no `[rand]` row and never will: entropy is the
// host's, and a `core` package that reached for it could not run in
// firmware.  Hand it 32 bytes from a source you trust — rand-nv, an
// nRF52's RNG peripheral, `/dev/urandom` — and every one of the 2²⁵⁶
// possibilities becomes a usable scalar, so the only refusal is the
// width.
//
// **The bytes are clamped here, not later.**  RFC 7748 §5's
// `decodeScalar25519` clears the low three bits and bit 255 and sets
// bit 254, and this constructor is where that happens, so a value of
// this type is always ready for the ladder.  The consequence a caller
// must know is on `secret_key_bytes`.
//
// ```novo
// use std.list
// use x25519
//
// fn main() [io]
//     var e: [u8] = []
//     for _i in 0..32
//         e = list.append(e, [9 as u8])
//     match x25519.secret_key_from_bytes(e)
//         Ok(_)  => println("a scalar")
//         Err(_) => println("refused")
// ```
pub fn secret_key_from_bytes(random_bytes: [u8]) -> Result<X25519SecretKey, X25519Error> []
    todo("x25519-nv.x25519.secret_key_from_bytes")

// The 32 bytes of a secret scalar, **as clamped**.
//
// NOT NECESSARILY THE BYTES THAT WENT IN.  `secret_key_from_bytes`
// clamps, so up to five bits of what a caller handed over are gone by
// the time they come back here.  That is the right way round — a
// stored key is then one nothing has to remember to clamp — but it is
// visible, and a caller that persists a key and compares it with its
// own entropy will see a difference.  Persist THIS, not the entropy.
//
// It is the one function here that hands out secret key material, and
// it exists because persisting a key is a real thing to want.
pub fn secret_key_bytes(k: X25519SecretKey) -> [u8] []
    todo("x25519-nv.x25519.secret_key_bytes")

// Read a peer's public key from 32 little-endian bytes.
//
// Checks the width and nothing else, and there is nothing else worth
// checking: bit 255 is masked away per RFC 7748 §5, a `u` at or above
// the field prime reduces, and a `u` that is not on the curve is on
// the twist, which is safe.  The low-order points are the one real
// hazard and `diffie_hellman` refuses their result rather than
// enumerating them here.
pub fn public_key_from_bytes(u: [u8]) -> Result<X25519PublicKey, X25519Error> []
    todo("x25519-nv.x25519.public_key_from_bytes")

// The 32 bytes of a public key, as they go on the wire.
pub fn public_key_bytes(k: X25519PublicKey) -> [u8] []
    todo("x25519-nv.x25519.public_key_bytes")

// Derive the public half of a key pair from a secret scalar.
//
// One ladder against the base point `u = 9`, which is
// `scalar_mult_base`.  It cannot fail — the base point is not a
// low-order point and a clamped scalar is never zero modulo the group
// order — so it is not a `Result`.
pub fn public_key_of(k: X25519SecretKey) -> X25519PublicKey []
    todo("x25519-nv.x25519.public_key_of")

// Build a key pair from 32 random bytes the caller supplies.
//
// `secret_key_from_bytes` followed by `public_key_of`, which is what a
// caller opening a session actually wants.
//
// ```novo
// use std.list
// use x25519
//
// fn main() [io]
//     var e: [u8] = []
//     for _i in 0..32
//         e = list.append(e, [9 as u8])
//     match x25519.key_pair_from_random(e)
//         Ok(kp) => println("${list.len(x25519.public_key_bytes(kp.public))}")
//         Err(_) => println("refused")
// ```
pub fn key_pair_from_random(random_bytes: [u8]) -> Result<X25519KeyPair, X25519Error> []
    todo("x25519-nv.x25519.key_pair_from_random")

// The raw scalar multiplication: `k · u`, as RFC 7748 §5 defines it.
//
// The Montgomery ladder, 255 steps, each one a conditional swap and a
// differential add-and-double, taking the same time for every scalar —
// which is what `fe25519.cswap` is for and why the scalar is clamped
// before it gets here.
//
// **This is the primitive, not the protocol.**  It does not refuse an
// all-zero result, because a caller reaching for this one has said it
// wants the arithmetic: a test vector, a hash-to-curve construction, a
// protocol that has its own contributory-behaviour rule.  For a key
// exchange, call `diffie_hellman`.
pub fn scalar_mult(k: X25519SecretKey, peer: X25519PublicKey) -> [u8] []
    todo("x25519-nv.x25519.scalar_mult")

// `k · 9`: the scalar multiplication against the curve's base point.
//
// The same ladder with `u = 9`, which RFC 7748 §4.1 fixes as the base
// point.  `public_key_of` is this with the result wrapped in a type.
pub fn scalar_mult_base(k: X25519SecretKey) -> [u8] []
    todo("x25519-nv.x25519.scalar_mult_base")

// The Diffie-Hellman shared secret between `k` and `peer`.
//
// `scalar_mult` with the low-order check made, and the difference is
// the whole reason both functions exist.
//
// **AN ALL-ZERO OUTPUT IS REFUSED.**  RFC 7748 §6.1 says: "protocols
// MAY check for the all-zero value" and "this check is not necessary
// for [some] protocols".  This package makes the check and returns
// `X25519SharedSecretIsZero`, for three reasons:
//
//   - An all-zero output means the peer sent one of the low-order
//     points.  The "shared" secret is then a constant that the peer
//     chose and already knows, so anything derived from it is known to
//     the peer too.
//   - The protocols for which the check is unnecessary are the ones
//     that authenticate the transcript afterwards, and they know who
//     they are; the ones that do not are the ones written by somebody
//     reaching for a Diffie-Hellman in a hurry.  A default that is safe
//     for the second group and merely redundant for the first is the
//     right default.
//   - A caller who genuinely wants the unchecked value has
//     `scalar_mult`, one line away, named for what it is.
//
// **The result is not a key.**  It is a `u` coordinate with structure
// in it, and RFC 7748 §6.1 says to run it through a key derivation
// function.  hkdf-nv's `extract` over it, with the handshake
// transcript as the salt, is what the protocols that get this right
// do.
//
// ```novo
// use std.list
// use x25519
//
// fn main() [io]
//     var e: [u8] = []
//     for _i in 0..32
//         e = list.append(e, [9 as u8])
//     match x25519.key_pair_from_random(e)
//         Err(_) => println("refused")
//         Ok(kp) =>
//             match x25519.diffie_hellman(kp.secret, kp.public)
//                 Ok(s)  => println("${list.len(s)}")
//                 Err(_) => println("a low-order peer key")
// ```
pub fn diffie_hellman(k: X25519SecretKey, peer: X25519PublicKey) -> Result<[u8], X25519Error> []
    todo("x25519-nv.x25519.diffie_hellman")

// Is `u` one of the points whose exchange yields the all-zero secret?
//
// The question `diffie_hellman` answers by refusing its own output,
// available separately for a caller that wants to reject a peer's key
// at the moment it arrives rather than at the moment it is used — a
// server storing a client's static key, for instance, which should not
// wait until a session to find out the key is worthless.
//
// There are five such `u` values modulo the prime — 0, 1, −1 and the
// two of order 8 — and more 32-byte encodings than that, because a
// value at or above the prime reduces: `p`, `p + 1` and `p − 1` are
// three more spellings of three of them.  Checking the OUTPUT stays
// the primary route, because it catches every case without a table and
// a table is a thing that can be missing an entry.
pub fn is_low_order(u: X25519PublicKey) -> Bool []
    todo("x25519-nv.x25519.is_low_order")
